  "body": "Many `HTMLMediaElement` properties and methods have corresponding media events, which can be used to monitor changes to the media state and update UI accordingly.\r\n\r\nE.g.\r\n* The `ratechange` event for `playbackRate`\r\n* The `volumechange` event for `muted` and `volume`\r\n* The `pause` event after `pause()` is called\r\n\r\nHowever there does not seem to be one in either the WHATWG or [W3C](https://www.w3.org/TR/html5/embedded-content-0.html#mediaevents) standards corresponding to the [`loop` property/attribute](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/embedded-content.html#attr-media-loop). This is a serious shame since it's common for media player UIs (especially for audio) to feature the ability to dynamically toggle single-track looping during playback. In fact, this is possible currently, but it is not easy to track that change if the client code is not privy to the operation that triggered the toggle.\r\n\r\nI would propose an event, possibly called `loopchange`, which would be fired whenever the `loop` attribute or property is modified.",
